__d(function(a,e,n,t,i,s,r){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Geography",textbook:"The Earth: Our Habitat \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:19,chapter_title:"Our Country \u2014 India",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["State India's total area and its rank in the world by size and population","Identify India's latitudinal and longitudinal extent, and the significance of the Tropic of Cancer","Name all seven countries that share a land border with India","Identify the water bodies that surround India on three sides","Explain India's Standard Meridian (82\xb030'E) and Indian Standard Time (IST)","Name the northernmost, southernmost, easternmost, and westernmost points of India","Describe India's six major physical divisions","Explain what makes India a subcontinent and a land of diversity","Connect Karnataka's location, neighbours, and physical features to the broader map of India"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Ability to read a simple map and understand compass directions (north, south, east, west)","Knowledge of latitude and longitude from earlier geography lessons","Awareness of landforms (mountains, plateaus, plains) from Chapter 18","Basic knowledge of India's major rivers and states from daily life"],introduction:{why_important:"India is our homeland. Knowing its size, shape, location, neighbours, and physical divisions is fundamental to understanding Indian history, culture, climate, and economy. Everything \u2014 from why India has monsoons to why certain crops grow in certain regions \u2014 is rooted in India's geography.",real_life_relevance:"When you use IST (Indian Standard Time) to check a timetable, you are using India's Standard Meridian (82\xb030'E). When you hear about tension at the 'Line of Actual Control', you are talking about India's border with China. When you enjoy Kodagu coffee, Mangaluru seafood, or Rajasthan's desert tourism, geography is shaping your experience. India's diversity is geography in action.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka is India's 6th largest state (191,791 sq km), located in southwestern peninsular India. It borders 6 states (Maharashtra, Goa, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana) and has a 320 km coastline on the Arabian Sea. Karnataka is on the Deccan Plateau, with the Western Ghats on its western border. Bengaluru, Karnataka's capital, is India's IT capital \u2014 a fact that itself reflects geography (plateau location = pleasant climate = comfortable workspace).",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapters 17 and 18 established Earth's domains and landforms. Chapter 19 applies these concepts specifically to India \u2014 placing India within the world map, understanding its physical divisions, and connecting its geography to daily life."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"India",role:"The seventh largest country by area and the most populous nation \u2014 a peninsula surrounded by the Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal, and Indian Ocean, with the Himalayas as a northern wall and a vast diversity of landscapes, climates, and cultures"},{name:"The Himalayas",role:"India's northern guardian \u2014 blocking cold Arctic winds, feeding the great northern rivers, and forming natural borders with China, Nepal, and Bhutan"},{name:"The Indian Ocean",role:"Named after India, the third largest ocean bounds India to the south and has historically made India a maritime trade powerhouse"},{name:"The Standard Meridian (82\xb030'E)",role:"India's time-keeper \u2014 the meridian that runs through Prayagraj and provides a uniform Indian Standard Time (GMT+5:30) for the entire country"}],plot:["The chapter opens by asking students to locate India on a world map \u2014 a peninsula jutting into the Indian Ocean from Asia's southern edge.","It establishes India's size (7th largest, 3.28 million sq km), population (most populous), and latitudinal-longitudinal extent.","It identifies India's seven land neighbours (Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar) and three sea neighbours (Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal, Indian Ocean).","It explains the Standard Meridian (82\xb030'E) and why India uses a single time (IST = GMT+5:30) despite spanning nearly 30\xb0 of longitude.","It describes India's six physical divisions: Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Indian Desert, Coastal Plains, and Islands.","It explores the concept of India as a 'subcontinent' \u2014 geographically distinct from the rest of Asia.","It covers India's island territories: Andaman & Nicobar (Bay of Bengal) and Lakshadweep (Arabian Sea).","It introduces the Thar Desert (Rajasthan) and the Tropic of Cancer as key geographic features.","The chapter closes by celebrating India as a 'land of diversity' \u2014 geographical diversity driving cultural, climatic, and agricultural variety."],moral:"India's geography is its greatest asset and its greatest challenge. A nation spanning deserts, mountains, rainforests, and coral islands must find unity in its extraordinary diversity. Understanding India's geography is the first step to understanding India's soul."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"India's Location, Size, and Extent",simple_explanation:"India is the 7th largest country in the world by area (3.28 million sq km) and the most populous. It lies between 8\xb04'N and 37\xb06'N latitude and 68\xb07'E to 97\xb025'E longitude. The Tropic of Cancer (23\xb030'N) passes through its middle.",detailed_explanation:"India's latitudinal extent of about 29 degrees gives it a wide variety of climates \u2014 from tropical in the far south (Kerala and Tamil Nadu near 8\xb0N) to subtropical and temperate in the far north (Ladakh at 35\u201337\xb0N). The longitudinal extent of about 29 degrees causes roughly a 2-hour difference in sunrise between Arunachal Pradesh (east) and Gujarat (west). The Tropic of Cancer passes through 8 Indian states: Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, and Mizoram. India occupies about 2.4% of the world's land area but houses about 18% of the world's population. India is bounded by the Himalayas in the north, the Arabian Sea in the west, the Bay of Bengal in the east, and the Indian Ocean in the south.",real_life_example:"Because India lies between 8\xb0N and 37\xb0N, the southernmost tip (Kanyakumari/Indira Point) experiences a tropical climate with year-round warmth, while Ladakh in the north experiences alpine cold with temperatures below \u221230\xb0C in winter. When you travel from Kerala to Ladakh, you cross virtually every climate zone on Earth \u2014 from tropical to arctic \u2014 all within one country.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka lies between approximately 11\xb0N and 18\xb0N latitude \u2014 firmly in the tropical zone. This means Karnataka has warm temperatures year-round. Karnataka's longitude (74\xb0E\u201378\xb0E) places it in the western part of the Indian landmass, close to the Arabian Sea, which is why the Southwest Monsoon reaches Karnataka's coast first (Mangaluru, around June 1\u20135) before moving inland. Karnataka's northern districts (Bidar, Kalaburagi) are more arid than the southern and western districts because they are further from the Ghats and the coast.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse the southernmost point of mainland India with the southernmost point of Indian territory. Kanyakumari (Tamil Nadu) is the southernmost tip of the mainland. But the southernmost point of India's territory is Indira Point on Great Nicobar Island (6\xb045'N). Similarly, students may not know that India's northernmost point is Indira Col in Siachen, Ladakh \u2014 not a city.",memory_tip:"Latitude: 8\xb0N (south tip, near Sri Lanka) to 37\xb0N (north, Siachen). Longitude: 68\xb0E (west, Gujarat) to 97\xb0E (east, Arunachal). Both extreme tips \u2014 north and south \u2014 are named 'Indira' (Indira Col and Indira Point). Standard Meridian: 82\xb030'E through Prayagraj. IST = GMT + 5 hours 30 minutes."},{concept_name:"India's Neighbours and Borders",simple_explanation:"India shares land borders with seven countries: Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, and Myanmar. It also has maritime (sea) neighbours: Sri Lanka and the Maldives. India's total land boundary is about 15,200 km and coastline is about 7,516 km.",detailed_explanation:"India's land neighbours clockwise from the northwest: Pakistan (northwest; India-Pakistan border includes the Radcliffe Line/International Boundary and the Line of Control in J&K); Afghanistan (tiny border section in the erstwhile J&K/POK area); China (north and northeast; India-China border is called the Line of Actual Control/LAC); Nepal (north; open border with free movement); Bhutan (northeast; friendly neighbour, no open border issues); Bangladesh (east; India's longest land border with any single country, about 4,156 km); Myanmar (east; sharing a border through Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland, and Arunachal Pradesh). Maritime neighbours: Sri Lanka (separated by the Palk Strait and connected by Adam's Bridge/Ram Setu); Maldives (coral island nation in the Arabian Sea, southwest of India).",real_life_example:"When you see news about India-Pakistan cricket matches, the tension reflects a political boundary that is also a geographic reality \u2014 two nations sharing the same subcontinent. When Bangladeshi or Nepali goods reach Indian markets, they cross land borders. When you eat Sri Lankan tea or Maldivian fish, you are consuming products from India's maritime neighbours across the Indian Ocean.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka does not share borders with any foreign country \u2014 it is an inland state surrounded by other Indian states. However, Karnataka's Arabian Sea coast (Dakshina Kannada, Udupi, Uttara Kannada) connects to the broader Indian Ocean trade network. Historically, the ports of Mangaluru, Honnavar, and Karwar were key nodes in trade with Arabia, East Africa, and Southeast Asia \u2014 all of which accessed India via the Indian Ocean. Understanding India's maritime boundaries helps explain Karnataka's coastal history.",common_mistakes:"Students often forget Afghanistan when listing India's neighbours \u2014 it shares a very small border section with India in the Gilgit-Baltistan/POK area, which is disputed. Also, students may list Sri Lanka as a land neighbour \u2014 it is NOT; Sri Lanka is separated from India by the Palk Strait (sea). Sri Lanka and Maldives are maritime (sea) neighbours only.",memory_tip:"Land neighbours clockwise from NW: 'Paki And China Never Bring Bangladesh Myanmar' \u2014 Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar (7 countries). Sea neighbours: Sri Lanka (east-south) and Maldives (southwest). Longest land border = Bangladesh (4,156 km). Longest coastline state = Gujarat."},{concept_name:"Standard Meridian and Physical Divisions of India",simple_explanation:"India's Standard Meridian is 82\xb030'E (passing through Prayagraj, UP), giving all of India one common time \u2014 IST = GMT+5:30. India is physically divided into six major zones: Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Indian Desert, Coastal Plains, and Islands.",detailed_explanation:"India spans about 29\xb0 of longitude (68\xb0E to 97\xb0E), which equals about 2 hours of time difference between east and west. To avoid this confusion, India adopted 82\xb030'E as its Standard Meridian \u2014 a centrally located line that passes through Naini, near Prayagraj (Allahabad), Uttar Pradesh. IST is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT). The six physical divisions: (1) Himalayan Mountains \u2014 young fold mountains in the north, source of rivers, climate barrier; (2) Northern Plains \u2014 vast alluvial plains of the Ganga, Indus, and Brahmaputra, most fertile and densely populated; (3) Peninsular Plateau \u2014 Deccan Plateau, ancient hard rock, mineral-rich; (4) Indian Desert \u2014 Thar Desert in Rajasthan, very low rainfall; (5) Coastal Plains \u2014 narrow strips along west (Konkan, Malabar) and east (Coromandel, Northern Circars) coasts; (6) Islands \u2014 Andaman & Nicobar (Bay of Bengal) and Lakshadweep (Arabian Sea).",real_life_example:"IST affects every Indian every day \u2014 all railway timetables, airline schedules, school timings, banking hours, and TV broadcasts run on IST. Without a single standard time, train schedules between Arunachal Pradesh and Gujarat would be chaotic \u2014 there would be a 2-hour difference in local solar time. India's single time zone, despite its wide span, is a practical necessity for national unity and coordination.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka falls entirely within the Peninsular Plateau division (the Deccan Plateau) and has a portion of the Western Coastal Plain (the stretch from Mangaluru to Goa). Karnataka also contains part of the Western Ghats \u2014 which serve as the ecological and physical boundary between the plateau interior and the coastal plain. Karnataka thus represents three of India's six physical divisions: plateau, coastal plain, and mountain (Western Ghats). This physical diversity within one state is reflected in Karnataka's linguistic diversity (coastal Tulu speakers, plateau Kannada speakers, tribal communities in the Ghats).",common_mistakes:"Students often think India has many time zones like the USA. India officially has only one time zone \u2014 IST. The half-hour offset (GMT+5:30 instead of a round GMT+5 or GMT+6) is unique to India and is a deliberate choice. Also, students may forget that India's island territories (Andaman & Nicobar and Lakshadweep) are the sixth physical division \u2014 they may list only five divisions.",memory_tip:"Six physical divisions: 'Himalayas Nourish People Despite Coastline Islands' \u2014 Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Desert (Thar), Coastal Plains, Islands. Standard Meridian: 82\xb030'E = 'eighty-two thirty' through Prayagraj. IST = GMT + 5:30 (India gets the sun 5.5 hours before London)."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Subcontinent",meaning:"A large, geographically distinct landmass that is part of a continent but separated from the rest by physical features. India is called a subcontinent because the Himalayas and surrounding seas isolate it from the rest of Asia."},{term:"Standard Meridian",meaning:"The meridian (line of longitude) used as the reference for a country's standard time. India's Standard Meridian is 82\xb030'E, which passes through Naini near Prayagraj."},{term:"Indian Standard Time (IST)",meaning:"The official time used across all of India, based on the Standard Meridian 82\xb030'E. IST is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT+5:30)."},{term:"Tropic of Cancer",meaning:"The parallel of latitude at 23\xb030'N that marks the northernmost point where the sun appears directly overhead (during summer solstice, June 21). It passes through 8 Indian states."},{term:"Peninsula",meaning:"A piece of land that juts into a body of water and is surrounded by water on three sides. India is a peninsula, bounded by the Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal, and Indian Ocean."},{term:"Palk Strait",meaning:"The narrow stretch of shallow water that separates India (Tamil Nadu) from Sri Lanka. Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu) \u2014 a chain of limestone shoals \u2014 runs across it."},{term:"Atoll",meaning:"A ring-shaped coral island or group of islands enclosing a shallow lagoon. Lakshadweep Islands are coral atolls in the Arabian Sea."},{term:"Indo-Gangetic Plain",meaning:"The vast, fertile alluvial plain in northern India formed by the rivers Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra. It is India's most densely populated region and its agricultural heartland."},{term:"Thar Desert",meaning:"The Great Indian Desert located in Rajasthan, receiving less than 25 cm of annual rainfall. It is the world's 7th largest desert and extends into Pakistan."},{term:"Line of Actual Control (LAC)",meaning:"The de facto boundary that separates Indian-controlled territory from Chinese-controlled territory. It is not a formally demarcated international border."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Map Work",question:"Identify the correct direction for each of India's neighbours: Where is Pakistan relative to India? Where is Bangladesh? Where is China?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Picture the map of India and use compass directions.","Step 2: Pakistan \u2014 located to the NORTHWEST of India, bordering the states of Gujarat, Rajasthan, Punjab, and J&K.","Step 3: Bangladesh \u2014 located to the EAST of India (and surrounded by India on three sides). It borders West Bengal, Meghalaya, Assam, Tripura, and Mizoram.","Step 4: China \u2014 located to the NORTH and NORTHEAST of India, bordering Ladakh, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Sikkim, and Arunachal Pradesh.","Step 5: Nepal \u2014 NORTH of India (between India and China), bordering UP, Bihar, West Bengal, Sikkim, and Uttarakhand.","Step 6: Myanmar \u2014 EAST of India, bordering Arunachal Pradesh, Nagaland, Manipur, and Mizoram.","Conclusion: Pakistan = NW. China = N/NE. Nepal = N. Bhutan = NE. Bangladesh = E. Myanmar = E. Afghanistan = NW (tiny border)."]},{example_number:2,type:"Reasoning",question:"India spans about 29 degrees of longitude but uses only one time zone. Is this reasonable? Why or why not?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Calculate the time difference \u2014 Earth rotates 15\xb0 per hour. 29\xb0 \xf7 15\xb0 = about 1.93 hours \u2248 2 hours difference between India's west (Gujarat) and east (Arunachal Pradesh).","Step 2: Compare with other countries \u2014 The USA (170\xb0 longitude span) uses 6 time zones. China (62\xb0 span) uses just 1 time zone. India (29\xb0 span) uses 1 time zone.","Step 3: Argument FOR one time zone \u2014 simplifies railway, aviation, banking, communication, and national administration. Avoids confusion in a country with one federal government and one railway network.","Step 4: Argument AGAINST \u2014 sunrise in Arunachal Pradesh is about 2 hours earlier than in Gujarat. Some northeastern states (like Assam and Arunachal) have informally used 'Chai Bagan Time' (tea garden time) \u2014 1 hour ahead of IST.","Conclusion: One time zone is a reasonable administrative choice for national unity and convenience, though it creates slight inconvenience for extreme eastern states. The decision prioritises national coordination over local accuracy."]},{example_number:3,type:"Application",question:"Classify each of Karnataka's geographic characteristics into one of India's six physical divisions.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall the six divisions \u2014 Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Indian Desert, Coastal Plains, Islands.","Step 2: Karnataka's Western Ghats (Sahyadri Range) \u2014 these are mountains running along Karnataka's western boundary = Mountain division (though technically part of the Peninsular Plateau system, they function as a mountain range in geography).","Step 3: The interior of Karnataka (Bengaluru, Mysuru, Hubballi, Dharwad, Vijayapura, Kalaburagi plateau region) = Peninsular Plateau (Deccan Plateau).","Step 4: The coastal strip (Dakshina Kannada, Udupi, Uttara Kannada) facing the Arabian Sea = Coastal Plains.","Step 5: Karnataka has no representation in the Northern Plains, Indian Desert, or Islands divisions.","Conclusion: Karnataka covers three of India's six physical divisions: Peninsular Plateau (majority of the state), Coastal Plains (western coast), and the Western Ghats (mountain boundary). This physical diversity within one state creates three distinct sub-regions with different climates, vegetation, and crops."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"India's Location, Size, Neighbours, and Physical Divisions",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is India's total area and its rank in the world by area?",a:"India's total area is about 3.28 million sq km. It is the 7th largest country in the world by area."},{type:"Recall",q:"Name the seven countries that share a land border with India.",a:"Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, and Myanmar."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why is India called a 'subcontinent'?",a:"India is called a subcontinent because it is a very large, geographically distinct landmass separated from the rest of Asia by the Himalayas in the north and surrounded by seas on three sides, making it almost like a mini-continent within the larger Asian continent."},{type:"Understanding",q:"What is India's Standard Meridian? What time does it give India?",a:"India's Standard Meridian is 82\xb030'E, passing through Naini near Prayagraj (Allahabad), UP. It gives India IST = GMT+5:30 (5 hours 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time)."},{type:"Application",q:"The Tropic of Cancer passes through which Indian state that is Karnataka's northern neighbour?",a:"Maharashtra does not have the Tropic of Cancer. The states through which the Tropic passes are Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, and Mizoram. Karnataka's northern neighbour Maharashtra is just south of the Tropic \u2014 the Tropic does not pass through it."}]}],key_takeaways:["India is the 7th largest country (3.28 million sq km) and the most populous in the world.","India lies between 8\xb04'N\u201337\xb06'N latitude and 68\xb07'E\u201397\xb025'E longitude.","The Tropic of Cancer (23\xb030'N) passes through 8 Indian states.","India has 7 land neighbours: Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar.","India is surrounded by the Arabian Sea (west), Bay of Bengal (east), and Indian Ocean (south).","India's Standard Meridian is 82\xb030'E (through Prayagraj); IST = GMT+5:30.","India has 28 states and 8 Union Territories (as of 2024).","India's six physical divisions: Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Indian Desert, Coastal Plains, Islands.","Karnataka is the 6th largest state, bordering 6 states, with 320 km of Arabian Sea coastline.","India's island groups: Andaman & Nicobar (Bay of Bengal) and Lakshadweep (Arabian Sea, coral atolls)."],mind_map:{root:"Our Country \u2014 India",branches:[{branch:"Location and Size",sub:["7th largest by area (3.28 million sq km)","Most populous country (2023)","8\xb04'N to 37\xb06'N; 68\xb07'E to 97\xb025'E","Tropic of Cancer (23\xb030'N) through 8 states"]},{branch:"Neighbours",sub:["Land (7): Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar","Sea: Sri Lanka (Palk Strait), Maldives (Arabian Sea)","Longest land border: Bangladesh (4,156 km)"]},{branch:"Standard Time",sub:["Standard Meridian: 82\xb030'E","Passes through Prayagraj (UP)","IST = GMT + 5:30","2-hour difference east to west"]},{branch:"Physical Divisions",sub:["Himalayan Mountains (north)","Northern Plains (Gangetic Plain)","Peninsular Plateau (Deccan)","Indian Desert (Thar, Rajasthan)","Coastal Plains (east & west coasts)","Islands (Andaman & Nicobar; Lakshadweep)"]},{branch:"Karnataka in India",sub:["6th largest state","Borders 6 states","320 km Arabian Sea coast","Peninsular Plateau + Western Ghats + Coastal Plain","Bengaluru = IT capital; Mangaluru = port city"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 19 covers India's geography. Area: 3.28 million sq km, 7th largest in world, most populous. Latitude: 8\xb04'N to 37\xb06'N; Longitude: 68\xb07'E to 97\xb025'E. Tropic of Cancer (23\xb030'N) passes through 8 states: Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, WB, Tripura, Mizoram. Land neighbours (7): Pakistan (NW), Afghanistan (NW), China (N/NE), Nepal (N), Bhutan (NE), Bangladesh (E), Myanmar (E). Longest border: Bangladesh (4,156 km). Maritime neighbours: Sri Lanka (Palk Strait, Adam's Bridge/Ram Setu), Maldives (Arabian Sea). Standard Meridian: 82\xb030'E through Naini, Prayagraj (UP). IST = GMT+5:30. 28 states + 8 UTs. Six physical divisions: Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains (food bowl), Peninsular Plateau (Deccan), Indian Desert (Thar, Rajasthan), Coastal Plains, Islands (Andaman & Nicobar = Bay of Bengal; Lakshadweep = Arabian Sea coral atolls). Karnataka = 6th largest state, 6 state borders, 320 km Arabian Sea coast. Extreme points: N = Indira Col (Siachen, Ladakh), S = Indira Point (Nicobar), E = Kibithu (Arunachal), W = Sir Creek (Gujarat). Southernmost mainland = Kanyakumari.",must_remember_facts:["India = 7th largest by area (3.28 million sq km), most populous country in world.","Latitude: 8\xb04'N to 37\xb06'N. Longitude: 68\xb07'E to 97\xb025'E.","Tropic of Cancer (23\xb030'N) passes through 8 states including Gujarat, MP, and West Bengal.","7 land neighbours: Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar.","3 water bodies: Arabian Sea (west), Bay of Bengal (east), Indian Ocean (south).","Standard Meridian: 82\xb030'E. IST = GMT+5:30. Passes through Prayagraj, UP.","28 states + 8 Union Territories (as of 2024).","Largest state by area: Rajasthan. Smallest state: Goa.","Andaman & Nicobar = Bay of Bengal. Lakshadweep = Arabian Sea (coral atolls).","Karnataka = 6th largest state, borders 6 states, 320 km Arabian Sea coastline."]}},2559,[]);